insert into public.questions (slug, title, category, summary, argument, critiques, standing, sort_order, reviewed_on) values
  ('intelligent-design', 'What is intelligent design, and is it science?', 'life', 'Intelligent design holds that some features of living things are best explained by an intelligent cause. Its proponents call it science; major scientific bodies and a 2005 federal court disagree.', 'Intelligent design (ID) is the claim that “certain features of the universe and of living things are best explained by an intelligent cause, not an undirected process such as natural selection” [1]. Its leading proponents, many of them fellows of the Discovery Institute’s Center for Science and Culture, include the philosopher of science Stephen Meyer, the biochemist Michael Behe and the mathematician and philosopher William Dembski.

Proponents distinguish ID from creationism. It makes no claim about the age of the earth or the identity of the designer, and Meyer writes that it does not challenge evolution as change over time, or even common ancestry; what it disputes is that the cause of biological change is wholly blind and undirected [2]. Meyer traces the modern argument to The Mystery of Life’s Origin (1984), in which Charles Thaxton and colleagues argued that the information-bearing properties of DNA pointed to a designing intelligence [2].

ID reasons by inference to the best explanation. Dembski argued that design can be detected when an event is both highly improbable and matches an independently given pattern, a combination he called specified complexity [4]. Proponents apply this to the information in DNA, to molecular machines that seem to need all their parts at once, to the sudden appearance of animal body plans in the Cambrian, and to the fine-tuning of physical constants. Because intelligent agents are known to produce such features, they argue, and no undirected process is known to, design is the better explanation [2].

Proponents insist the argument is positive, not merely a gap in current theories. Meyer writes that design theorists argue for design not only because chance, natural law or the two together have failed to explain certain features, but because “we know from experience that systems possessing these features invariably arise from intelligent causes” [2]. On this view, inferring design from biological information is no different in kind from an archaeologist inferring a carver from an inscription, or the search for extraterrestrial intelligence treating a coded radio signal as the work of a mind [2].', 'The central scientific objection is that ID offers no mechanism or testable predictions of its own: its arguments point to what evolutionary theory has not explained and conclude design. In Kitzmiller v. Dover Area School District (2005), after a six-week trial with expert witnesses on both sides, federal judge John E. Jones III found that ID “is at bottom premised upon a false dichotomy, namely, that to the extent evolutionary theory is discredited, ID is confirmed” [3].

Critics also contest each specific argument, as the related questions describe, and scientific bodies have rejected ID as science. In 2002 the American Association for the Advancement of Science resolved that “the lack of scientific warrant for so-called ‘intelligent design theory’ makes it improper to include as a part of science education” [5].

Philosophers who study design arguments note a long-standing difficulty with arguments of this kind: much of their historical persuasiveness came from the absence of any known natural explanation, and when such explanations appear, the argument loses its force [6]. Critics see the same pattern in ID. Proponents reply that they argue from what is known about the causes of information and machinery, not from what is unknown [2].

A further objection concerns ID’s origins. Comparing drafts of the ID textbook Of Pandas and People from before and after the Supreme Court’s 1987 ruling against teaching creationism as science, the Kitzmiller court found that the early drafts’ definition of creation science was identical to the later definition of ID, and that about 150 uses of “creationism” and its cognates had been systematically replaced [3]. Proponents answer that a theory’s history does not decide whether it is true, and that ID’s claims differ from creationism’s [2].', 'ID has not been accepted as science by the major scientific organizations, and the Kitzmiller ruling barred teaching it in that district’s public-school science classes [3][5]. Its proponents point to work they have published in peer-reviewed journals and monographs [1], and argue that its rejection reflects a definition of science that excludes design in advance. A few philosophers who are not theists, such as Bradley Monton, have argued that ID deserves a hearing as science even if its conclusions turn out to be false [7].

Whether nature shows evidence of design is one of the oldest questions in philosophy, and the arguments remain under active discussion [6]. Whether ID’s particular arguments count as science, philosophy or theology is itself contested, including among Christians, many of whom accept evolution and see God’s purposes in natural processes rather than in gaps in them [6].', 1, '2026-10-25'),
  ('dna-and-information', 'Does the information in DNA point to a designer?', 'life', 'Stephen Meyer argues that the digital code in DNA is best explained by intelligence, since information of that kind always comes from minds. Critics answer that the argument rests on gaps in origin-of-life research and a disputed notion of information.', 'DNA stores the instructions for building proteins in a four-character chemical code. Just as letters convey a message by their arrangement, Meyer writes, particular sequences of bases along DNA convey precise instructions for building proteins, the property Francis Crick’s “sequence hypothesis” described [1].

Meyer’s argument, developed at length in Signature in the Cell (2009), concerns the origin of the first living cell, before natural selection could operate [2]. He argues that chance is far too improbable to produce the information in even a minimal cell, that chemical laws cannot explain it because DNA’s sequence is not dictated by chemical attraction between its parts, and that no theory of undirected chemical evolution has explained it [1].

Meyer treats the possible explanations in turn. Chance alone, he argues, is ruled out by the vast number of possible sequences compared with the tiny fraction that produce working proteins. Chemical necessity is ruled out because the bases can attach along the DNA backbone in any order, which is what lets DNA carry information in the first place. Combinations of chance and selection, he argues, cannot help before there is a self-copying system for selection to act on, and he contends that proposals such as the RNA world presuppose the very information they are meant to explain [1][2].

His case is meant to be positive, not only a gap. Drawing on William Dembski’s concept of specified complexity [3], he argues that in our uniform experience, information of this kind arises from minds: a computer program from a programmer, a newspaper from a writer [1]. He compares the inference to the search for extraterrestrial intelligence, which would treat an information-bearing radio signal from space as evidence of an intelligent source [1].', 'Critics’ first objection is that the argument rests on what origin-of-life research has not yet explained. That research is unfinished, as its practitioners say [5], but critics read its progress, including recent advances in systems chemistry [5], as reason to keep looking for a natural explanation rather than to conclude design.

Second, critics dispute the concept of information. Wesley Elsberry and the computer scientist Jeffrey Shallit argued that Dembski’s “complex specified information” is not a well-defined quantity that can be calculated for biological systems, and that his arguments contain significant errors [3][4]. Shallit also points to experiments and simulations in which evolutionary processes increase information, contrary to Dembski’s claims [3].

Third, critics question the analogy behind the inference. The information in human artifacts comes from minds we already know exist and can observe at work; the origin of life was a one-time event in the remote past. Extending the analogy to an unobserved designer, they argue, goes beyond what experience can support, an objection to design arguments that goes back to David Hume [6].

Philosophers note that design arguments have historically drawn much of their force from the absence of a known natural explanation, and lost it when one was found [6]. Critics expect the same here as origin-of-life chemistry advances. Meyer answers that his argument rests on what is known about the causes of information, not only on what is unknown [1].', 'How life began remains unsolved; Addy Pross and Robert Pascal call it “one of the more challenging scientific questions of all time” [5]. Meyer’s argument addresses a real open question, and his critics agree on that much. They disagree over whether an unsolved problem is evidence for design or a research program still under way.

Meyer’s design inference has not been adopted in origin-of-life research, and Dembski’s measure of specified information has not been taken up in information theory [3][4]. Proponents argue that this reflects the field’s commitment to finding only natural causes [2].', 2, '2026-10-25'),
  ('irreducible-complexity', 'Are some molecular machines irreducibly complex?', 'life', 'Michael Behe argues that systems like the bacterial flagellum need all their parts at once, so they could not have evolved step by step. Biologists answer that parts can be borrowed from other systems, and point to the flagellum’s relatives.', 'In Darwin’s Black Box (1996), the biochemist Michael Behe defined an irreducibly complex system as “a single system which is composed of several well-matched, interacting parts that contribute to the basic function, wherein the removal of any one of the parts causes the system to effectively cease functioning” [1][2]. His best-known example is the bacterial flagellum, a rotary motor bacteria use to swim; others include the blood-clotting cascade and parts of the immune system [2].

Behe’s argument takes up a test Darwin himself proposed: if any complex organ could be shown that “could not possibly have been formed by numerous, successive, slight modifications, my theory would absolutely break down” [4]. Natural selection can only preserve what already works, Behe argues, so a precursor missing a part would give selection nothing to favor; an irreducibly complex system therefore cannot be produced directly by slight, successive modifications [2]. He concludes that such systems are better explained by design [6].

Behe pointed to the flagellum because it is a true rotary motor, with a rotor, a stator, a drive shaft and a propeller made of protein, and because knocking out its proteins one at a time leaves bacteria unable to swim [1][6]. In 1996 Behe also claimed that science would never find an evolutionary explanation for the immune system [2].', 'Biologists’ main answer is that evolution need not build a system by adding parts toward its present function. Parts can be borrowed from systems that did something else, a process called co-option or exaptation, and parts that once helped can later be lost. Behe’s definition, critics say, rules out only the direct route [3][5].

For the flagellum, Mark Pallen and Nicholas Matzke reported that many of its proteins share ancestry with proteins in other systems: its export machinery with the type III secretion system some bacteria use to inject proteins into host cells, its motor proteins with other membrane transport systems, and its ATPase with the F- and V-type ATPases found across life [3]. At the Kitzmiller trial, the biologist Kenneth Miller presented peer-reviewed studies identifying possible precursors of the flagellum and evolutionary explanations for the clotting cascade and the immune system, and the court accepted them [2][5].

The immune system became a turning point at the Kitzmiller trial. On cross-examination Behe was presented with fifty-eight peer-reviewed publications, nine books and several immunology textbook chapters on the evolution of the immune system, and said they were not “good enough” as evidence. The court concluded that the ID argument depends on setting a scientifically unreasonable burden of proof for evolution [2]. Behe’s position is that such studies describe what might have happened without showing, step by step, that it did, and that this is the evidence he asked for [6].

Proponents reply that shared ancestry is not a pathway: showing that parts resemble parts elsewhere does not show how the whole was assembled, or that each intermediate stage worked and could be selected [6].', 'The Kitzmiller court observed that, unlike ID as a whole, irreducible complexity is testable, since it is refuted by showing intermediate structures with selectable functions [2]. Most biologists regard it as answered in principle for the flagellum and the clotting cascade.

Detailed step-by-step histories remain incomplete; Pallen and Matzke wrote that the flagellar research community “has scarcely begun to consider how these systems have evolved” [3]. Behe maintains that the proposed pathways are speculative [6].', 3, '2026-10-25'),
  ('cambrian-explosion', 'Does the Cambrian explosion challenge evolution?', 'life', 'Most major animal groups appear in the fossil record within a geologically short span beginning about 539 million years ago. Stephen Meyer argues undirected evolution cannot explain it; paleontologists answer that it was fast but not too fast.', 'Beginning about 539 million years ago, the fossil record shows the rapid appearance of most major animal phyla, the basic body plans of animal life, including arthropods, mollusks and the ancestors of vertebrates [5]. Darwin recognized this as a difficulty. Of the absence of earlier fossils he wrote, “The case at present must remain inexplicable; and may be truly urged as a valid argument against the views here entertained” [1].

In Darwin’s Doubt (2013), and earlier in a 2004 review article, Stephen Meyer argues that the explosion is not only a problem of missing fossils but of missing information: building many new body plans required new genes, new proteins and new developmental programs in a short span, and he argues that mutation and selection could not generate so much novel information so quickly [2][6]. He concludes that the explosion is best explained by intelligent design [2].

Meyer adds two further arguments. Animal body plans are set up early in development by networks of regulatory genes, and mutations to those early networks are almost always fatal, so he argues that the networks resist the gradual change evolution would need [2]. And the fossil pattern runs “top down”: the big differences between body plans appear first, with the variety within each plan filling in afterward, which he argues is the opposite of what a gradual branching process would predict [2].', 'Paleontologists answer that the explosion was neither instantaneous nor without antecedents. The Ediacaran organisms lived in the tens of millions of years before it, and trace fossils record burrowing animals before the main radiation [5]. A 2018 review of the arthropod record by Allison Daley and colleagues concluded that the group’s diversification “unfolded gradually over the ∼40 million years of the lower to middle Cambrian” [4].

Molecular clocks, estimates of when lineages split based on genetic differences, suggested from the 1990s that animal lineages began diverging before the Cambrian. That prompted searches of older rocks, which have since turned up at least 30 new Ediacaran fossil sites [4]. Paleontologists disagree over how far back the animal lineages go, but most read the evidence as a long fuse lit before an explosion that the fossil record exaggerates, because hard parts that fossilize well arose in the Cambrian [4][5].

Reviewing Darwin’s Doubt in Science, the paleontologist Charles Marshall argued that Meyer overstated how much new genetic material the explosion required: animal body plans are built largely by rewiring developmental gene networks, many of whose genes already existed before the Cambrian, rather than by inventing many new genes [3].

Critics also point to explanations for why the radiation happened when it did, including rising oxygen levels, new ecological relationships such as predation, and the evolution of developmental gene networks [5].', 'The speed of the Cambrian radiation is real and still studied; paleontologists debate its triggers and how much of its suddenness reflects which animals could fossilize [4][5]. Meyer’s conclusion, that it requires design, has not been accepted in paleontology. Proponents answer that the proposed triggers describe conditions for the radiation without explaining where the information it required came from [2].', 4, '2026-10-25'),
  ('fine-tuning', 'Is the universe fine-tuned for life?', 'universe', 'Physicists find that small changes to several constants and initial conditions would make life impossible. Whether that points to a designer, a multiverse or nothing at all is debated.', 'Many of the constants and conditions that describe our universe appear to fall in narrow ranges that permit life. The physicist Luke Barnes summarizes the claim: “in the space of possible physical laws, parameters and initial conditions, the set that permits the evolution of intelligent life is very small” [1]. Examples include the relative strengths of the fundamental forces, the masses of elementary particles, and the cosmological constant, the energy of empty space, whose observed value is far smaller than simple theoretical estimates [1][3].

Hoyle’s own example concerned carbon, the basis of all known life. Stars make carbon and oxygen through a sequence of nuclear reactions that depends on a specific energy level in the carbon nucleus, at 7.65 MeV, and on one in oxygen. Hoyle noted that if you wanted to produce carbon and oxygen in roughly equal quantities in stars, these are just the two levels you would have to fix, and that they sit just about where they are found [2].

The astronomer Fred Hoyle, who was not religious, concluded from his work on how stars make carbon that “a superintellect has monkeyed with physics, as well as with chemistry and biology” [2]. The philosopher Robin Collins develops the theistic argument: a life-permitting universe is far less surprising if a designer intended one than if the constants were set by chance, so fine-tuning counts as evidence for design [5].', 'One response is the anthropic principle: we could only ever observe a universe compatible with our existence. As the physicist Brandon Carter put it, our location in the universe is necessarily privileged to the extent of being compatible with our existence as observers [3]. On its own this explains why we see a life-permitting universe, not why there is one.

Another is the multiverse. If there are many universes with different constants, as some versions of inflationary cosmology and string theory suggest, life-permitting ones are bound to occur somewhere [3]. Critics of this response note that other universes cannot be observed, and some philosophers argue that inferring a multiverse from our one life-permitting universe commits an “inverse gambler’s fallacy” [3].

Others question the premise. Victor Stenger argued that the life-permitting range is much wider than claimed when several constants are varied together [6]; Barnes replied in detail that most cases of fine-tuning survive that objection [1]. Some philosophers doubt that probabilities can be meaningfully assigned to constants that could take any value [3], and Sean Carroll argues that we know too little about which universes could support some form of life, and about what a designer would do, for fine-tuning to favor theism [4].

Some critics also question what the argument shows even if fine-tuning is real. The philosophers Elliott Sober and Jan Narveson argue that we are in no position to predict what a cosmic designer would prefer, so we cannot say that design makes a life-permitting universe more likely than chance [3]. Theistic philosophers have replied that a good God has reason to create embodied creatures like us, and the exchange continues [3].', 'That some constants are finely tuned in this sense is widely accepted among physicists, though how many and how finely is debated [1][3]. What explains it is not something physics currently settles: design, a multiverse, an undiscovered deeper law and brute fact all have serious defenders, and the choice among them rests largely on philosophical arguments about probability and explanation [3].', 5, '2026-10-25'),
  ('cosmological-argument', 'Did the universe need a cause?', 'universe', 'The kalam cosmological argument reasons that whatever begins to exist has a cause, and the universe began to exist. Modern cosmology bears on the second premise; both premises are disputed.', 'The kalam cosmological argument, named for the medieval Islamic theologians who developed it and revived by the philosopher William Lane Craig, runs in three steps: whatever begins to exist has a cause of its beginning; the universe began to exist; therefore the universe has a cause of its beginning [1].

Craig defends the first premise as a principle confirmed by all experience: things do not come into being without a cause [1]. For the second he offers philosophical arguments against an infinite past and scientific evidence: the expansion of the universe traced back to the Big Bang, and a 2003 theorem by Arvind Borde, Alan Guth and Alexander Vilenkin showing that a universe that has on average been expanding cannot be extended infinitely into the past [1][4]. He then argues that a cause of all space and time must itself be beyond them: timeless, immaterial, enormously powerful and personal [1][2].

Craig’s philosophical case for the second premise turns on the difference between a potential infinite, a series that can always be extended but is never complete, and an actual infinite, a completed infinite collection. Following the medieval argument, he holds that an actual infinite cannot exist in reality and cannot be built up one moment at a time, so the series of past events must be finite and the universe must have begun [1].', 'Critics challenge the first premise. Our experience of causes comes from things beginning within the universe, and whether it applies to the universe as a whole is the point in question. Some appeal to quantum physics, in which individual events such as radioactive decays have no deterministic cause [2]. Craig replies that quantum events still have causal conditions, and that a quantum vacuum is not nothing [2].

Others accept that the past might be finite but question the reasoning against an infinite one. Modern mathematics treats actual infinities as consistent, and critics argue that the paradoxes Craig uses, such as a hotel with infinitely many rooms that is full yet can take new guests, are strange but not contradictory [2].

Others challenge what a beginning would mean. If time itself began at the Big Bang, there was no earlier moment at which a cause could act; Adolf Grünbaum argued on this ground that asking for a cause of the Big Bang is a pseudo-question [2][6]. The Big Bang model also describes the universe’s early expansion rather than necessarily an absolute beginning, since general relativity is expected to break down at the earliest times, and physicists have proposed models without a first moment [2][5].

Sean Carroll argues that cosmologists take both “beginning” and “eternal” models seriously, and that which is true should be settled by which models best fit the evidence, not by metaphysical principles about causes [5].', 'Cosmologists agree that the observable universe has expanded from a hot, dense early state. Whether that was an absolute beginning is open: the theorem Craig cites rules out some eternal models but not all, and a full theory of quantum gravity may change the picture [2][3]. Vilenkin and Audrey Mithani, examining the main eternal scenarios, concluded that the answer is “probably yes” [3].

The first premise, and the step from a first cause to a personal God, are philosophical questions, and philosophers remain divided over both [2].', 6, '2026-10-25')
on conflict (slug) do update set title = excluded.title, category = excluded.category,
  summary = excluded.summary, argument = excluded.argument, critiques = excluded.critiques,
  standing = excluded.standing, sort_order = excluded.sort_order, reviewed_on = excluded.reviewed_on;

delete from public.questions where slug not in ('intelligent-design', 'dna-and-information', 'irreducible-complexity', 'cambrian-explosion', 'fine-tuning', 'cosmological-argument');

-- Children are replaced wholesale so removed entries disappear too.
delete from public.question_claims where question_id in (select id from public.questions where slug in ('intelligent-design', 'dna-and-information', 'irreducible-complexity', 'cambrian-explosion', 'fine-tuning', 'cosmological-argument'));
delete from public.question_quotes where question_id in (select id from public.questions where slug in ('intelligent-design', 'dna-and-information', 'irreducible-complexity', 'cambrian-explosion', 'fine-tuning', 'cosmological-argument'));
delete from public.question_sources where question_id in (select id from public.questions where slug in ('intelligent-design', 'dna-and-information', 'irreducible-complexity', 'cambrian-explosion', 'fine-tuning', 'cosmological-argument'));
delete from public.related_questions where question_id in (select id from public.questions where slug in ('intelligent-design', 'dna-and-information', 'irreducible-complexity', 'cambrian-explosion', 'fine-tuning', 'cosmological-argument'));

insert into public.question_claims (question_id, position, claim, rating, explanation)
select q.id, v.position, v.claim, v.rating, v.explanation
from (values
  ('intelligent-design', 1, 'Living things contain intricate systems that look designed.', 'established', 'Biologists and design proponents agree that living systems are intricate and functional and can look designed; the appearance of design is the starting point of design arguments from William Paley onward [6]. The dispute is over what explains it.'),
  ('intelligent-design', 2, 'Intelligent design is a scientific theory.', 'debated', 'The major scientific bodies and the Kitzmiller court concluded that it is not, chiefly because it proposes no testable mechanism [3][5]. Where science ends is a question in the philosophy of science, and some philosophers, not all of them theists, argue the line is harder to draw than critics allow [6][7].'),
  ('intelligent-design', 3, 'Intelligent design grew out of creationism.', 'supported', 'The Kitzmiller court documented that the first ID textbook was revised from creationist drafts after the 1987 Supreme Court ruling [3]. Proponents point to earlier roots in Thaxton’s 1984 work on DNA and argue that ID’s claims differ from creationism’s, since it accepts an old earth and does not identify the designer [2].'),
  ('intelligent-design', 4, 'Design is the best explanation for these features of life.', 'debated', 'This is an inference about explanation, which no experiment settles. Proponents argue intelligence is the only known cause of such features [2]; critics argue evolutionary explanations already account for most of them and that ID gains its support only from gaps in current knowledge [3].'),
  ('dna-and-information', 1, 'DNA stores information in a code-like sequence.', 'established', 'Biologists describe DNA as carrying genetic information: the order of its four bases specifies the sequence of amino acids in proteins, as Crick’s sequence hypothesis proposed [1]. The disagreement is over what the information shows about origins, not over the description.'),
  ('dna-and-information', 2, 'No undirected chemical process has been shown to produce a living cell.', 'established', 'No complete pathway from simple chemistry to a living cell has been demonstrated, and researchers describe the origin of life as unsolved [5]. That is a statement about current knowledge; it does not show that no such pathway exists.'),
  ('dna-and-information', 3, 'Natural processes cannot produce new genetic information.', 'contradicted', 'Experiments and simulations show evolutionary processes generating new information and novelty [3]. This popular form of the argument goes beyond Meyer’s own, which concerns the first cell, before selection could act [2].'),
  ('dna-and-information', 4, 'The information in DNA is best explained by an intelligent cause.', 'debated', 'This is an inference to the best explanation, which no experiment settles. Proponents argue intelligence is the only known source of such information [1]; critics argue the inference depends on gaps in current knowledge and stretches the analogy with human design [3][6].'),
  ('irreducible-complexity', 1, 'Removing a part from some molecular machines stops them working.', 'established', 'This is the observation behind Behe’s definition, and it is not in dispute for systems like the flagellum [1][2]. The question is what it implies about how the system arose.'),
  ('irreducible-complexity', 2, 'Irreducibly complex systems could not have evolved step by step.', 'contradicted', 'The claim assumes a precursor missing a part would be useless. Flagellar proteins have relatives that do other jobs in other systems, showing that parts can be useful apart from the whole [3], and the Kitzmiller court accepted peer-reviewed evidence of precursors [2]. Complete pathways have not been worked out [3].'),
  ('irreducible-complexity', 3, 'Irreducibly complex systems are best explained by design.', 'debated', 'This is an inference about explanation, which no experiment settles. Behe argues that design is the only known cause of such systems [6]; critics argue that co-option offers a natural one and that the design inference rests on gaps in pathway details [3][5].'),
  ('cambrian-explosion', 1, 'Most animal phyla first appear in the fossil record during the Cambrian, in a geologically short time.', 'established', 'This is the observation all sides start from [4][5]. How short is debated: estimates for the main radiation run from about 20 to 40 million years, depending on what is counted [4].'),
  ('cambrian-explosion', 2, 'The Cambrian animals appeared with no precursors.', 'contradicted', 'Ediacaran organisms and animal burrows precede the main radiation [5]. Many individual phyla, though, have no deep Precambrian record; for arthropods, Daley and colleagues found none [4].'),
  ('cambrian-explosion', 3, 'Undirected evolution could not produce the Cambrian body plans in the time available.', 'unverified', 'Meyer argues this from estimates of the new information required [2][6], but no published analysis has shown that the time was insufficient, and paleontologists consider the radiation’s tens of millions of years consistent with known evolutionary processes [3][4].'),
  ('cambrian-explosion', 4, 'The Cambrian explosion is best explained by intelligent design.', 'debated', 'This is an inference about explanation, which no experiment settles. Meyer argues intelligence is the only known source of the information required [2]; critics argue that existing genes, rewired, and changing environments account for the radiation [3][5].'),
  ('fine-tuning', 1, 'Several physical constants fall in narrow ranges that permit life.', 'supported', 'Detailed reviews find many such cases [1], and most physicists accept the core observation. Which constants count, how narrow the ranges are, and whether they can vary independently remain debated [1][3][6].'),
  ('fine-tuning', 2, 'A multiverse explains fine-tuning.', 'unverified', 'Some cosmological theories predict many universes with different constants, but none has been observed, and whether one could be is disputed; philosophers also debate whether a multiverse would explain our universe’s fine-tuning at all [3].'),
  ('fine-tuning', 3, 'Fine-tuning shows the universe was designed.', 'debated', 'This is an inference about probability and explanation, which no experiment settles. Collins and others argue design makes fine-tuning far more likely [5]; critics argue we cannot assign the probabilities the argument needs [3][4].'),
  ('cosmological-argument', 1, 'The universe expanded from a hot, dense early state.', 'established', 'The Big Bang model is the accepted account of the universe’s early history, though it describes the expansion, not what if anything came before it [2][5].'),
  ('cosmological-argument', 2, 'The universe had an absolute beginning.', 'debated', 'Theorems such as Borde–Guth–Vilenkin rule out some eternal models [4], and Mithani and Vilenkin judge a beginning probable [3]. Other physicists defend eternal models, and the physics of the earliest moments is not yet understood [2][5].'),
  ('cosmological-argument', 3, 'Whatever begins to exist has a cause.', 'debated', 'Craig defends it as confirmed by all experience [1]; critics question whether experience within the universe extends to the universe itself, and whether quantum events are exceptions [2].'),
  ('cosmological-argument', 4, 'The cause of the universe is a timeless, personal being.', 'debated', 'This is a philosophical inference, which no experiment settles. Craig argues only a personal agent could freely produce a temporal effect from a timeless cause [1]; critics argue the inference does not follow, or that the question of a cause does not arise [2][6].')
) as v (slug, position, claim, rating, explanation)
join public.questions q on q.slug = v.slug;

insert into public.question_quotes (question_id, position, quote, speaker, role, source_position)
select q.id, v.position, v.quote, v.speaker, v.role, v.source_position
from (values
  ('intelligent-design', 1, 'The theory does not challenge the idea of evolution defined as change over time, or even common ancestry, but it does dispute Darwin’s idea that the cause of biological change is wholly blind and undirected.', 'Stephen C. Meyer', 'Philosopher of science, Discovery Institute', 2),
  ('intelligent-design', 2, 'We have addressed the seminal question of whether ID is science. We have concluded that it is not, and moreover that ID cannot uncouple itself from its creationist, and thus religious, antecedents.', 'Judge John E. Jones III', 'Kitzmiller v. Dover (2005)', 3),
  ('dna-and-information', 1, 'We know generally that information—whether inscribed in hieroglyphics, written in a book or encoded in a radio signal—always arises from an intelligent source.', 'Stephen C. Meyer', 'Philosopher of science, Discovery Institute', 1),
  ('dna-and-information', 2, 'Artificial life researchers regularly find their simulations of evolution producing the sorts of novelties and increased complexity that Dembski claims are impossible.', 'Jeffrey Shallit', 'Computer scientist, University of Waterloo', 3),
  ('irreducible-complexity', 1, 'If it could be demonstrated that any complex organ existed, which could not possibly have been formed by numerous, successive, slight modifications, my theory would absolutely break down.', 'Charles Darwin', 'On the Origin of Species (1859)', 4),
  ('irreducible-complexity', 2, 'An irreducibly complex system cannot be produced directly by slight, successive modifications of a precursor system, because any precursor to an irreducibly complex system that is missing a part is by definition nonfunctional.', 'Michael J. Behe', 'Biochemist, Lehigh University (quoted in Kitzmiller v. Dover)', 2),
  ('irreducible-complexity', 3, 'Like Darwin, we have found that careful attention to homology, analogy and diversity yields substantial insights into the origin of even the most complex systems.', 'Mark Pallen and Nicholas Matzke', 'Microbiologist and evolutionary biologist', 3),
  ('cambrian-explosion', 1, 'The case at present must remain inexplicable; and may be truly urged as a valid argument against the views here entertained.', 'Charles Darwin', 'On the Origin of Species (1859)', 1),
  ('cambrian-explosion', 2, 'Rather than being a sudden event, this diversification unfolded gradually over the ∼40 million years of the lower to middle Cambrian, with no evidence of a deep Precambrian history.', 'Allison C. Daley and colleagues', 'Paleontologists, PNAS (2018)', 4),
  ('fine-tuning', 1, 'A common sense interpretation of the facts suggests that a superintellect has monkeyed with physics, as well as with chemistry and biology, and that there are no blind forces worth speaking about in nature.', 'Fred Hoyle', 'Astronomer, University of Cambridge', 2),
  ('fine-tuning', 2, 'In the space of possible physical laws, parameters and initial conditions, the set that permits the evolution of intelligent life is very small.', 'Luke A. Barnes', 'Physicist, Western Sydney University', 1),
  ('fine-tuning', 3, 'Our location in the universe is necessarily privileged to the extent of being compatible with our existence as observers.', 'Brandon Carter', 'Physicist (1974), quoted in the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy', 3),
  ('cosmological-argument', 1, 'Whatever begins to exist has a cause of its beginning.', 'William Lane Craig', 'Philosopher, Talbot School of Theology', 1),
  ('cosmological-argument', 2, 'Did the universe have a beginning? At this point, it seems that the answer to this question is probably yes.', 'Audrey Mithani and Alexander Vilenkin', 'Physicists, Tufts University', 3),
  ('cosmological-argument', 3, 'There is no event—the beginning of the universe—to be explained, events being possible only in time', 'Bede Rundle', 'Philosopher, University of Oxford (quoted in the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)', 2)
) as v (slug, position, quote, speaker, role, source_position)
join public.questions q on q.slug = v.slug;

